1. What is Multithreading?

Multithreading is the ability of a program to execute multiple threads concurrently to maximize CPU utilization.
A thread is a lightweight sub-process
Part of the same process
Shares memory (heap)

Interview one-liner
“Multithreading allows concurrent execution of tasks within a single process.”

2. Why Multithreading?
| Benefit          | Explanation                      |
| ---------------- | -------------------------------- |
| Performance      | Better CPU utilization           |
| Responsiveness   | Non-blocking UI / APIs           |
| Scalability      | Parallel task execution          |
| Resource sharing | Lower memory cost than processes |

3. Process vs Thread (🔥 Very Common)
| Process             | Thread              |
| ------------------- | ------------------- |
| Heavyweight         | Lightweight         |
| Own memory          | Shared memory       |
| Slow context switch | Fast context switch |
| Independent         | Dependent           |

4. Thread Creation in Java (2 Ways)
4.1 Extending Thread Class
class MyThread extends Thread {
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Thread running");
    }
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyThread t = new MyThread();
        t.start();
    }
}

Output
Thread running

4.2 Implementing Runnable (Preferred ✅)
class MyTask implements Runnable {
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Runnable thread");
    }
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Thread t = new Thread(new MyTask());
        t.start();
    }
}

Output
Runnable thread

Why Runnable is Better?

✔ Supports multiple inheritance
✔ Better design
✔ Preferred in enterprise apps

5. Thread Lifecycle (🔥 MUST-KNOW)
NEW → RUNNABLE → RUNNING → WAITING/BLOCKED → TERMINATED

States:
NEW – Thread created
RUNNABLE – Ready to run
RUNNING – CPU executing
WAITING / BLOCKED – Waiting for resource
TERMINATED – Execution completed

6. Important Thread Methods
t.start();      // start thread
t.run();        // executes like normal method
t.sleep(ms);    // pause execution
t.join();       // wait for thread to finish
t.getName();
t.setPriority();

7. start() vs run() (🔥 Tricky)
t.start(); // creates new thread
t.run();   // no new thread

Interview Tip:
“Calling run() does NOT create a new thread.”

8. Thread Priorities
Thread.MIN_PRIORITY  // 1
Thread.NORM_PRIORITY // 5
Thread.MAX_PRIORITY  // 10

⚠ JVM may ignore priorities (OS dependent)

